- Java source audit: `NettyRemotingClient.start()` installs `defaultEventExecutorGroup` for handlers unless `disableNettyWorkerGroup` is true, while socket I/O remains on the event loop. `registerProcessor(..., null)` chooses the same `publicExecutor` used as the default callback executor; shutdown calls `publicExecutor.shutdown()`. Updated Rust routing so configured default processors share the callback worker queue, task admission is serialized with shutdown, and post-shutdown submissions reject; direct inbound mode bypasses processor workers. This still has different Tokio thread/queue/shutdown lifecycle semantics and remains PARTIAL.
- Implemented bounded per-connection frame-buffer reuse (maximum pool length four), `encode_into`, RAII return of unqueued frames, return after completed/failed writes, and queue failure recycling. Tests prove capacity reuse, closed/full queue recovery and pool bound. Individual write-error/cancel/shutdown buffer retention is not asserted; lifecycle remains a parity difference from Netty's global pooled allocator.
